Neurodegeneration is a complex process that involves the dysregulation of a plethora of biomolecules which ultimately leads to abnormal cellular activity and behavioral changes.15
Among such diseases, Parkinson’s disease (PD) is characterized by the loss of dopaminergic neurons and the aggregation of $\alpha$ -synuclein in Lewy bodies.16,17 Despite limited therapeutic options, one common practice involves the use of levodopa as a dopamine (DA) replacement to mitigate bradykinetic symptoms.18 Thus, methods that can detect DA levels in biological samples is of high importance.19,20 Numerous methods have been developed to assess neurodegeneration by quantifying the DA levels, including superresolution microscopy,21 mass spectrometry imaging,22 electrochemical measurements,23 and so forth. Among them, electrochemical methods can quantitatively measure multiple neurotransmitters and exhibit good sensitivity for detecting DA released from cells.24,25 In this endeavor, recent studies have focused on developing in vitro electrochemical platforms that can detect differential DA responses between the physiological and pathological conditions.
We recently reported the synthesis of a novel class of crystalline structure organic−inorganic hybrid molecule,
NTOU-7, which is composed of indium atoms, phosphate ligands, and 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(imidazol-1-ylmethyl)benzene linkers. The results showed that NTOU-7 exhibit not only an interesting crystalline structure and high chemical stability, but also display good sensitivity for the detection of ractopamine, which is a controversial food additive in livestock reported to raise health concerns at high concentrations (Supporting Information 1.1).26 Regarding materials that have been demonstrated for DA sensing, many studies have reported the use of benzene-based metal−organic framework (MOF) molecules, including MIL-101(Cr),27 HKUST-1,28,29 Al-MIL$53\mathrm{-}\big(\mathrm{OH}\big)_{2},^{30}$ MOF-525,11 and Abtz- $\operatorname{CdI}_{2}$ -MOF.31 These reported MOF DA sensors showed substantially enhanced performance via the incorporation of additional materials, including conducting polymers and plasmonic nanoparticles, to form hybrid materials. The typical dynamic range and limit of detection (LOD) reported by these materials ranges from 0.5 nM to $1~\mathrm{mM}$ and $0.11\ \mathrm{nM}$ to $1.5\ \mu\mathrm{M},$ respectively. Based on these results and upon inspection of structural features of NTOU-7, it is reasoned that NTOU-7 might also have the capacity for sensing DA. As such, this study showcased the implementation of NTOU-7 as an integral part of a hybrid microfluidic sensor for DA detection, which critically depends on the compatibility of the hybrid materials with the existing synthetic protocols and fabrication methods of microfluidic chips. It is imperative to focus on a fundamental understanding of the properties of hybrid materials and to improve the limitations of electrochemical biosensors, as designing a compact and integrated biosensor for sensitive and reliable DA electrochemical measurement is of substantial important yet remains a challenge.
In view of the advantages of a microfluidics biosensor and chemical stability and sensing potential of NTOU-7, we report herein on the development of a NTOU-7-integrated microfluidic chip featuring the in situ biosensor chip-compatible polyaniline (PANI) synthesis to form the nanointerface (N7- P) for sensing DA with high specificity and sensitivity (LOD at the attomolar range; Figure 1A). To demonstrate the biological application, the performance of this biosensor was examined by detecting DA secreted directly from mass-limited live cell samples [with 21 neuro-2A (N2a) cells]. Furthermore, structural and electrochemical investigations relating to various parameters during nanointerface synthesis, as well as for the optimization of overall device sensing performance, were also systematically conducted.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Figure 1B,C shows the schematic and photograph of the microfluidic sensor constructed with a micro-channel as a flow layer and N7-P modified electrode as a sensing layer, as well as the working principle of the proposed electrochemical DA sensing. A flow layer made with polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) was constructed with three inlets and one outlet using a soft lithography method (Figure S1). The reagent flow was controlled using a syringe pump operated at a flow rate of $50~\mu\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ (Supporting Information 1.2). Meanwhile, an indium tin oxide (ITO) substrate was used as the sensing layer, onto which a three-electrode system was custom fabricated using a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ laser (Figure S1). The working electrode ( $\mathrm{~i~mm~}$ in diameter) was functionalized with N7-P to serve as the sensing module. To minimize the particle size of NTOU-7 in order to achieve an increased surface area and sensing performance, the as-synthesized NTOU-7 powder was subjected to additional “stirring” treatment for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ after the original synthetic route.32 The resulting particles, named N7 hereafter, was characterized and found to exhibit decreased and smaller sizes ( $257\pm42.2\mathrm{nm},$ ) and be more homogeneous than that of the pristine NTOU-7, as confirmed by the dynamic light scattering analysis (Figure S2A and Supporting Information 1.1).
Electrochemical characterizations showed that the electric charge (C), electron transfer (ET) rate, and response to DA of N7 were enhanced by 21.2, 27.2, and $23.3\%$ , respectively, when compared to the pristine NTOU-7 (Figure S2B−D). N7 was thus used to form hybrid nanointerface with PANI for the subsequent experiments (Supporting Information 2.1 and Figure S3). Subsequently, the sweep potential was used for the in situ electrochemical polymerization of aniline with N7 at the electrode surface. This method allows for an accurate control of the electrochemical deposition and thickness of resulting nanointerface (Supporting Information 1.3). Brightfield time lapse images showed the color switching between green and dark blue during the polymerization reaction, confirming the formation of the emeraldine base and the pernigraniline base, which represents as a key feature for the electrochemical PANI synthesis (Figure 1D).33,34 Based on such result, the N7-P nanointerface was prepared using the cyclic voltammetry (CV) under an optimal condition, which involves the use of $1\ \mathrm{mg}\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}$ of N7 with $50~\mathrm{mM}$ of aniline and 30 scanning cycles with a scan rate of $50\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ from $-0.2$ to 1.0 V (Figures 1E, S4A−D and Supporting Information 2.2). Notably, the N7-P nanointerface was found to exhibit elevated DA sensing performance compared to the bare, N-7, and PANI modified electrodes (Figure S4E). Moreover, the morphology of the N7-P nanointerface was characterized by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements (Supporting Information 1.5). With 30 scanning cycles, PANI was found to form a uniform “coating” around N7 to exhibit a core−shell nanostructure (Figure 1F). On the other hand, SEM images acquired at different scanning cycles (5, 10, and 50) during PANI synthesis revealed either an under- or over-growth of PANI around N7 (Figure S5). For control samples, SEM images of the pure N7 and PANI showed irregular-shaped morphology with the particle size of ${\sim}300~\mathrm{nm}$ and fiber-like aggregated particles, respectively (Figure S5A,B). Altogether, these results confirm that N7-P grew on the electrode via a controllable, scanning cycledependent electrochemical polymerization.
Figure 2A shows the powder X-ray diffraction (P-XRD) patterns of the N7, PANI, and N7-P on the ITO surface (Supporting Information 1.5). The XRD spectra of N7 and N7-P were in good agreement with the previous characterizations and simulated single-crystal XRD analysis of NTOU-7, indicating that the crystalline structure of N-7 remained intact after PANI electropolymerization. The P-XRD analysis of PANI exhibited no noticeable peaks, which is indicative of the amorphous structure.32 Meanwhile, the Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectrum of N7-P shows characteristic signals from both N7 and PANI, suggesting that a hybrid material was synthesized successfully (Figure 2B). The characteristic bands for N7 were observed at 654 and $1613~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponding to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ of the aromatic ring, $757~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the −OH group, 1088 and $11\bar{0}6~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponding to the imidazole $\mathrm{ring}^{35}$ and 1251 and $1352~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the $\mathrm{C-N}$ bonding. For PANI, the signals at 744 and $823~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were observed, which corresponds to $\mathrm{C-H}$ out-of-plane bending in the benzenoid ring. The bands observed at 1102 and $1307~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ as well as $1\mathsf{\breve{s}s}8\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ correspond to $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretching vibration in the aromatic region and $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretching mode, respectively.32
To study the charge resistance and ET at the interfaces, we performed electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements on the bare screen-printed carbon electrode (SPCE), N7, PANI, and N7-P functionalized electrode. Figure 2C shows the Nyquist plots of bare SPCE and various modified electrodes recorded (Supporting Information 1.4). Compared to the relatively large charge-transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ of the bare SPCE $\left(3.8\dot{4}\mathrm{k}\Omega\right)$ , N7 exhibited a substantially decreased $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ value of $0.761~\mathrm{{\k}}\Omega$ . This implies that N7 functionalization alone improved the ET property. Notably, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of N7-P decreased markedly by ${\sim}20$ times when compared to the bare SPCE, to a value of $0.191\ \mathrm{\k}\Omega,$ confirming that PANI further enhances the ET kinetics at the nanointerface. The obtained EIS data were also fitted to an equivalent electrical circuit model to generate relevant parameters that will be useful for the future device integration (Figure 2C insert and Table S1). Next, the ET process and reaction kinetics of these samples were systematically investigated by CV operated under different scanning rates. The redox peak currents of N7-P increased linearly with the scan rates ranging from 50 to $800\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (Figure S6A). The linear plot between peak currents $\cdot I_{\mathrm{{pa}}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{pc}})$ and the square root of the scan rate $\left(\nu^{1/2}\right)$ indicated a typical diffusioncontrolled and quasi-reversible process (Figure 2D).36 Further analysis allowed for the determination of the peak-to-peak separation $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{p}})$ value for the as-synthesized NTOU-7, N7, and N7-P (Figure S6B). The $\Delta E_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ value is used to evaluate the reversibility of ET kinetics between the N7-P modified electrode and the analytes $\left(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+/3+}\right)$ .36 Consistent with EIS measurement showing improved ET rates, N7-P showed smaller $\Delta E_{\mathrm{{v}}}$ $\mathrm{'187.1\mV)}$ and lower barrier at a fast scan rate of $500\ \mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}}$ . In contrast, both NTOU-7 and N7 showed larger $\Delta E_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ (602.5 and $550~\mathrm{mV},$ ), confirming a slower ET rate at the electrode surface.37 Altogether, the results suggest that N7-P exhibits efficient charge transport property that should facilitate the DA detection.
Following systematic examinations of various structural and electrochemical properties of N7-P, we used differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) to benchmark its DA sensing capability. DPV measurements of samples containing different concentrations of DA were performed, and the recorded current responses are shown in Figure 3A (Supporting Information
1.4). At the level of $10^{-9}$ to $10^{-5}$ M DA, N7-P shows reasonably good sensing linearity $\cdot\mathbf{R}^{2}$ of 0.97). Note that this level of sensitivity is sufficient for evaluating DA levels in the human blood and serum sample, as the average concentrations of which were reported to be in the range of $\overline{{10}}^{-9}$ to $10^{-6}\mathrm{M}$ .38 Moreover, five neurotransmitters, including acetylcholine (Ace), epinephrine (EP), norepinephrine (NEP), gammaaminobutyric acid (GABA), and serotonin (Ser), were used to examine the sensing specificity of N7-P. DPV responses from these samples clearly showed good selectivity of N7-P toward DA, despite structural similarities of these molecules that may pose challenges in distinguishing them by the electrochemical method (Figure 3B).39,40 Furthermore, FTIR was used to understand the sensing mechanism between N7-P and DA. As shown in Figure 3C, the light blue and green blocks indicate discernible signals between DA and N7-P. Specifically, the spectrum of DA showed characteristic bands at $1207~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponding to the $\mathrm{C-H}$ bending vibration, $1500~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponding to $\mathrm{O-H}$ vibration stretching of diols, 1619 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ in-plane bending, and 1600 and $1633~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponding to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ of the benzene ring.41 In addition, the spectrum of the DAnanointerface complex showed a bending vibration peak redshifted from 1338 to $1368~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (solid triangles), suggesting that a strong hydrogen bonding association led to peak broadening.42 The band for the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ in the N7-P/DA showed a significantly lower frequency (1582 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}.$ ) than that in N7-P $\left(1613~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right)$ ) because of strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions (hashtag marked).41 Taken together, these data suggest that the affinity between $\mathsf{N}7{\cdot}\mathsf{P}$ and DA is mainly mediated by the hydrogen bonding and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions.
Next, we evaluated the reliability and stability with respect to the N7-P sensing. As shown in Figure S7A, CV curves measured from three independently-prepared N7-P microfluidics sensors were almost indistinguishable, suggesting that the synthesis and performance of N7-P sensor is robust and reproducible (Supporting Information 2.3). Furthermore, the structural stability of the nanointerface was tested at different $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ conditions by CV. The result shows a linear plot illustrating formal potentials $\left(E^{0}\right)$ as a function of $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ values ranging from 1 to 13 (Figure S7B). The fitted slope of ${\mathfrak{s o}}\mathrm{mV}$ · $\mathsf{p H}^{-1}$ agrees well with the theoretical value calculated based on the Nernst equation, which indicates that there is a balanced proton and ET at the nanointerface.43,44 Meanwhile, the assay stability was conducted by performing continuous CV scans for $12\mathrm{h}$ ( $>1000$ cycles) under a stirring condition $\left(400~\mathrm{rpm}\right)$ . The results showed no noticeable drop in the recorded signals; the original activity was maintained at $\sim99\%$ during the entire duration (Figure S7C). Overall, these results indicate that the N7-P microfluidic device was stable and durable across a wide $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range and can last for at least 1000 scanning cycles.
One unique feature of the microfluidics biosensor is its ability to create a dynamic (i.e., flowing) sensing modality using a relatively small amounts of samples. Compared to most conventional “flow-less“ electrochemical sensors, such a sensing strategy is known to substantially increase sensing performance since it has been shown to effectively enhance mass transport and lower background currents.45−49 As such, we used the amperometry ( $\dot{\iota}-t$ curve) method to examine the sensitivity of the N7-P biosensor toward DA detection under both the static and dynamic conditions (Supporting Information 2.4 and Figure S8). The results obtained under the static (i.e., no-flow) condition showed that N7-P can detect DA ranging from $1\ \mathsf{p M}$ to $10~\mathrm{{nM}}$ (Figure 4A). Notably, the
Since the reported concentration of DA released from single vesicle of rat pheochromocytoma (PC12) cells was at the ${\sim}1.9$ $\times\ 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{mol\ievel},^{24}$ we wonder if the N7-P biosensor can be applied to detect DA released directly from mass limited input cells, e.g., $<100$ cells. As a benchmark to confirm DA levels from the mouse neuroblastoma N2a cells, we also used a commercial enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) DA kit (Supporting Information 1.6, 1.7 and Figure S10). As shown in Figure 5A, the results demonstrated an almost indistinguishable performance regarding the detection of DA released from cells upon KCl stimulations between the commercial kit and N7-P biosensor. These results indicate that the N7-P nanointerface was reliable and capable of detecting DA levels released from stimulated neural cells. Note that a major difference between the commercial kit and N7-P nanointerface is their sensitivity; the commercial kit has an analytical LOD of ${\sim}49~\mathrm{pg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ (approximately $0.32~\mathrm{nM}$ ), and that of the N7-P nanointerface is $\hat{1.83}\times10^{-19}\mathrm{M}$ . Indeed, our attempt to determine DA released from a low seeding density (<1000 cells $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ) using the commercial ELISA kit failed to report any signals (Figure S10B and insert). In contrast, the same analyte processed by the N7-P biosensor successfully sensed DA signals released (Figure S11 and insert).
Based on these encouraging results, we subsequently prepared samples containing a series of low numbers of N2a cells (21−1667 cells) and used N7-P to directly quantify their DA levels upon 0.1 M KCl stimulation. Notably, Figure 5B shows the calculated DA concentration from as low as 21−83 cells, whose values were found to be at the ∼tens of aM range. Further analysis of the 21-cell data showed that the average numbers of DA $(N_{\mathrm{DA}})$ released from individual N2a cells is approximately $160\pm2.3\times10^{3}$ molecules, which is consistent with that of single PC12 cell reported previously $\mathrm{\mathit{N}_{D A}}=73.2$ $\pm\:5.82\times10^{3}$ molecules).25 In addition, the result also showed that estimated DA concentrations from 209 to 1667 cells were at the ${\sim}7.5~\mathrm{pM}-0.25~\mathrm{nM}$ range (Figure 5C). A comparison of these results with those obtained using high-performance liquid chromatography analysis showed a similar level of DA detected from N2a cells, which is $4.9\pm0.5\mathrm{{\pM}}$ 50 Further experiments containing either harpagide, a natural product known to promote synaptic DA release,51,52 or 6-hydroxydopamine (6-OHDA),53,54 a neurotoxicant that is detrimental to dopaminergic neurons, were performed to verify the capacity of N7-P for detecting DA released from cells under these treatments. We treated N2a cells with either harpagide or 6- OHDA and used N7-P integrated biosensor to measure corresponding DA levels (Supporting Information 1.6 and). Figure 5D,E shows measured DA concentrations upon N2a cells treated with various conditions. As expected, the release of DA was increased by ${\sim}2.9$ -fold upon KCl stimulation. Meanwhile, DA released from cells treated with harpagide was significantly higher than that of other groups. Conversely, upon 6-OHDA treatment, ${\sim}17$ and $66\%$ decrease in the DA level was observed when compared with cells treated with KCl and harpagide, respectively, indicating its neurotoxicity effect on N2a cells. Taken together, these results indicate that N7-P is a reliable DA sensor capable of sensing DA released from as low as 21 N2a cells, and it can be applied to differentiate DA released under different experimental conditions.
## CONCLUSIONS
In this study, we report the development of a novel indium phosphate-PANI nanointerface, N7-P, as an integral part of microfluidics biosensor for DA detection. Chemical stability and sensing characteristics of N7 enabled us to devise and optimize synthetic protocols compatible with the on-chip electrochemical synthesis of the N7-P nanointerface. Importantly, a variety of structural and sensing characterizations clearly showed that N7-P is a promising DA sensor. Particularly, our results showed that N7-P exhibits a markedly wide dynamic range $\left(10^{-18}-10^{-11}\mathrm{\:M}\right)$ and excellent sensitivity $\mathrm{\small{(LOD=0.183~aM)}}$ . Moreover, practical bioanalytical utility of $\mathrm{_{N7-P}}$ microfluidics biosensor successfully demonstrated its superior capability for detecting DA released from as low as 21 N2a cells. Looking forward, further developments of the system, such as by on-chip integration of cell culture units and miniaturization of overall sensing scheme, are expected to elevate sensing performance to the next level, e.g., to enable single-cell content analysis. Moreover, the chip-compatible synthetic strategy introduced here can be widely applicable to synthesize other hybrid materials or incorporate additional functional materials. This shall allow for the development of advanced point-of-care devices for diverse analytes with superior sensitivity and functionality.
